{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgER9okGivjjA5biNexPe", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_018hV2StjtrxkaiBycicxzqQ", "name": "WebSearch", "input": {"query": "Buginese closest relatives Campalagian Tamanic languages Embaloh Taman Kalimantan", "mode": "standard"}, "caller": {"type": "direct"}}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1372, "cache_read_input_tokens": 5536, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1372}, "output_tokens": 8, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-21T00:14:22.678Z", "request_id": "req_011CgER9oVPbhkDxJaRnzdR9", "wire_tool_inputs": {"toolu_018hV2StjtrxkaiBycicxzqQ": {"query": "Buginese closest relatives Campalagian Tamanic languages Embaloh Taman Kalimantan", "mode": "standard"}}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_018hV2StjtrxkaiBycicxzqQ", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buginese_language\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamanic_languages\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embaloh_language\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Languages_of_Sulawesi\"}, {\"url\": \"https://wikipedia.com/wiki/ISO_639:emb\"}, {\"url\": \"https://raco.cat/index.php/Dialectologia/article/download/427266/521774/628251\"}, {\"url\": \"https://nli.org.il/en/authorities/987007556356505171\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.prayingforindonesia.com/?p=2087\"}, {\"url\": \"https://finto.fi/lexvo/en/page/emb\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.prayingforindonesia.com/ethnic-groups/the-people-of-sulawesi/who-are-the-campalagian/\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-21T00:14:31.936Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "Buginese closest relatives Campalagian Tamanic languages Embaloh Taman Kalimantan", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_01M8XpR4uG55QaJ4XrGLGP2L", "content": [{"title": "Buginese language", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buginese_language"}, {"title": "Tamanic languages", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamanic_languages"}, {"title": "Embaloh language", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embaloh_language"}, {"title": "Languages of Sulawesi", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Languages_of_Sulawesi"}, {"title": "Embaloh language", "url": "https://wikipedia.com/wiki/ISO_639:emb"}, {"title": "raco.cat", "url": "https://raco.cat/index.php/Dialectologia/article/download/427266/521774/628251"}, {"title": "Embaloh language", "url": "https://nli.org.il/en/authorities/987007556356505171"}, {"title": "www.prayingforindonesia.com", "url": "https://www.prayingforindonesia.com/?p=2087"}, {"title": "Concept information", "url": "https://finto.fi/lexvo/en/page/emb"}, {"title": "Praying for Indonesia", "url": "https://www.prayingforindonesia.com/ethnic-groups/the-people-of-sulawesi/who-are-the-campalagian/"}]}, "The search results broadly support the picture that Buginese sits in the South Sulawesi branch, with Campalagian and the Tamanic languages of West Kalimantan as its nearest relatives.\n\n- **Buginese:** Within South Sulawesi, Buginese is most closely related to Campalagian and to the Tamanic outlier in West Kalimantan (Within the South Sulawesi subgroup, it is most closely related to Campalagian and the Tamanic outlier in West Kalimantan.).\n- **Tamanic languages:** These are spoken in Kapuas Hulu Regency, West Kalimantan, by the Embaloh and Taman Dayak subgroups (Embaloh (incl. Kalis) and Taman (Taman Dayak).). They are not closely related to other Borneo languages and most probably form one branch with Buginese (The Tamanic languages are not closely related to other languages on Borneo. Instead, they belong to the South Sulawesi languages, most probably in one branch together with Buginese.).\n- **Embaloh:** Among South Sulawesi languages, Embaloh is the one closest to Buginese (Apart from Taman, it is not close to other languages on Borneo, but rather belongs to the South Sulawesi languages closest to Buginese.). A Finto/Lexvo entry notes its classification has long been disputed: Adelaar and Himmelmann (2005) place it especially close to Buginese, but a 2008 lexicostatistical study treats it as an isolate within Nuclear Malayo-Polynesian (Apart from Taman, it is not close to other languages, and its classification has long been controversial. Adelaar and Himmelmann (2005) propose that it was especially close to Buginese. However, it is frequently left as an isolate within (Nuclear) Malayo-Polynesian, a position supported by a 2008 lexicostatistical study.).\n- **Campalagian:** Its classification places it in the Bugis group alongside Buginese (Bugis: Buginese, Campalagian). A community description also places Campalagian in the Bugis family, which includes Bugis in South Sulawesi as well as the Dayak Embaloh and Dayak Taman in Kalimantan (Their language, also called Campalagian, is part of a larger linguistic grouping called the Bugis family. This group includes the Bugis in the province of South Sulawesi, as well as the Dayak Embaloh and the Dayak Taman in Kalimantan (Borneo).).\n- **Taman caveat:** A 2024 paper by Chong Shin distinguishes two Taman languages. Tamanic Taman is historically related to South Sulawesi languages, while Sekadau Taman is a Malayic variety (Historically, Tamanic Taman is related to the languages in South Sulawesi, whereas Sekadau Taman is a Malayic variety.).\n\nOverall, the sources agree that Buginese, Campalagian, Embaloh, and Tamanic Taman form a South Sulawesi (Bugis–Tamanic) grouping. The main disagreement is over how tightly Embaloh is tied to Buginese, and the 2008 study in the Finto entry is the only source I found questioning that."], "durationSeconds": 9.125741999999999, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_018hV2StjtrxkaiBycicxzqQ", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgERAjs8X7faNsDxz1XdA", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "", "signature": ""}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1771, "cache_read_input_tokens": 6908, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1771}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-21T00:14:41.417Z", "request_id": "req_011CgERAjXo1ocHUkFDHgJVK", "thinking_duration_ms": 7239, "thinking_display": "updates"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgERAjs8X7faNsDxz1XdA", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**答案：** 布吉斯语（Bahasa Bugis）关系最近的是**坎帕拉吉安语（Campalagian）**和**塔曼语支（Tamanic）**。坎帕拉吉安语在西苏拉威西使用；塔曼语支在西加里曼丹，包括 Embaloh 语和 Taman 语。这几种语言都属于南方岛语系下的南苏拉威西语群，同在其中的 Bugis–Tamanic 分支。\n\n两点说明：\n- 塔曼语支归在南苏拉威西语群，是 Adelaar 和 Himmelmann（2005）的观点。2008 年有一项词汇统计研究把 Embaloh 语当作孤立语言，所以这个归类并非没有争议。\n- 第一次搜索查到一篇只比较了 9 种印尼语言的论文，结论是巴厘语离布吉斯语最近。那篇论文只看了论文里的 9 种语言，不能代表普遍的分类，所以没有采用。\n\n我搜了 3 次。\n\n来源：\n- [Buginese language – Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buginese_language)\n- [Tamanic languages – Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamanic_languages)\n- [Embaloh language – Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embaloh_language)\n- [Campalagian language – Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Campalagian_language)\n- [Embaloh – Finto/Lexvo](https://finto.fi/lexvo/en/page/emb)\n- [Praying for Indonesia – Campalagian](https://www.prayingforindonesia.com/ethnic-groups/the-people-of-sulawesi/who-are-the-campalagian/)\n- [Subgrouping dan Migrasi Sembilan Bahasa di Indonesia (UNY)](https://journal.uny.ac.id/index.php/humaniora/article/view/5369)"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1771, "cache_read_input_tokens": 6908, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 1771}, "output_tokens": 5, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-21T00:14:41.426Z", "request_id": "req_011CgERAjXo1ocHUkFDHgJVK"}
